{"task":"Add ldev rehome, to move a WordPress site onto its portless URL","goals":"Make the portless URLs the consolidation created actually usable by WordPress sites set up under the old ported one","status":"Waiting","recorded_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:24Z","body":"Collapsing the serving modes gave every site a portless URL, but a WordPress site\nset up under the old per-site mode does not accept one. It records its own address,\nso fronting seasonal-drops.ldev on 443 produced a 301 straight back to\nhttps://seasonal-drops.ldev:8443 — a real WordPress response, proving the proxy\nworked and the site still disagreed about where it lived.\n\nTwo places have to agree. The WP_HOME/WP_SITEURL constants in wp-config.php win when\npresent, and the database carries the old URL through post content, options, menus and\nwidget settings. Changing only the constants gives you a site that loads and then\nserves every image and link from a port that no longer answers, which reads as a\nbroken theme rather than a stale URL.\n\nThe database half goes through `wp search-replace`, never SQL, and that is the reason\nwp-cli is a hard requirement for this one command. WordPress stores arrays as\nPHP-serialized strings whose length prefixes are counted in BYTES. Replacing a longer\nURL with a shorter one inside such a string leaves the prefix overstating the content:\nPHP reads the declared length, overshoots, and hands back false. Every plugin reading\nthat option then silently reverts to defaults, and nothing about the symptom points at\na URL change made days earlier.\n\nBecause this rewrites site data rather than ldev's own files, the order is deliberate:\n\n  - count first, and print it — seasonal-drops comes to 16,224 replacements, which\n    deserves a different answer than \"1\" and is worth knowing before consenting\n  - refuse to proceed without a terminal to confirm on, unless --yes is explicit\n  - export the database and copy wp-config.php BEFORE either half is touched\n  - constants before search-replace, so a failure halfway leaves the site reachable\n    at the working URL and fixable by hand; the reverse ordering does not\n  - print the two commands that undo it\n\n--skip-columns=guid because a post's guid is an identifier, not a link: feed readers\nkey on it, and rewriting it makes every existing post look new.\n\nThe tests cover what it refuses rather than the happy path, which needs a live\ndatabase: an unknown site, a directory with no wp-config.php, a name that cannot be a\nhostname, a site already portless (asserting it writes no backup and modifies no file),\nand a real move with no tty (asserting wp-config.php is untouched).\n","parent_commit":"cc7088c11237331a6501ddb8989c140efc57b14f","repo":"brave-cedar-fb33","pct":"93","tasks":[{"id":"diagnose","content":"Diagnose sites.ldev: persite mode, nothing on 80/443, no wildcard fallback","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"bare-dirs","content":"Serve bare directory names in the wildcard template","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"list","content":"Make ldev list report bare dirs and flag unreachable names","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"mode","content":"Decide how to change this machine: consolidate to one mode","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"consolidate","content":"Consolidate the three modes into one","status":"completed","children":[{"id":"proxy-front","content":"Wildcard server fronts a site running its own server","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"apply-gen","content":"ldev render/apply generates a proxy block per standalone site","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"admin-port","content":"Site admin ports start above the wildcard server's","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"loop-guard","content":"Refuse to front a site claiming port 80 or 443","status":"completed"},{"id":"drop-modes","content":"Remove the install-time mode question; per-site becomes ldev standalone","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"},{"id":"apache","content":"Drop apache mode, whose template was never committed","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"}]},{"id":"wp-urls","content":"WordPress sites pinned to :8443","status":"completed","children":[{"id":"rehome","content":"ldev rehome: count, confirm, back up, rewrite both halves, print the undo","status":"completed"},{"id":"rehome-tests","content":"Guard tests: unknown site, non-WordPress dir, bad name, already-portless no-op, no-tty refusal","status":"completed"},{"id":"rehome-run","content":"Run ldev rehome against the three real WordPress sites","status":"pending","human_reason":"Rewrites live site data, not ldev's own files: 16,224 database rows for seasonal-drops alone, plus WP_HOME/WP_SITEURL in wp-config.php. The command deliberately refuses to run without a terminal to confirm on, so an agent cannot execute it and should not be given --yes on someone's real databases. It backs up the database and copies wp-config.php before touching either half, and prints the two commands that undo it. Bryce runs it per site: seasonal-drops, matsu, matsu-dev. The tool itself is delivered and tested in 130f207."}]},{"id":"docs","content":"README and docs/standalone-sites.md rewritten for the single mode and rehome","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z"}],"agent":"claude-code","changelog":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","code_quality":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","vulnerabilities":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","code_style_suggestions":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","reusability":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","system_compatibility":"Unreviewed (agent 